Design: one shared truth table in core/synthesis_context.context_matches —
both identities known -> identical fingerprint payloads only (profile, model
revision, language, voice/clone and generation settings all change the audio);
stored identity unknown -> reusable only for a VieNeu request
(legacy_render_compatible), because every pre-multi-engine render was VieNeu's;
requested identity unknown (no seam on the app, or a combination the capability
table refused) -> only another unknown identity may reuse it. A mismatch is
never a licence to substitute an engine: the audiobook marks the chapter failed
with the capability table's own message and the subtitle render refuses with it.
context_from_payload is the fail-soft inverse of fingerprint_payload (unknown
profile, blank/wrong-typed revision, unsupported combination, non-dict payload
-> None = "no recorded identity"), so a corrupt or hand-edited workspace
degrades to a re-render instead of failing the load.

Audiobook: state.json gains a "renders" map of payload-per-chapter-index.
load_book(context=) re-reconciles ready -> pending for a chapter whose recorded
identity cannot serve the requested one, and BookState.contexts exposes the
records; callers that do not render (progress, export, chapter text) omit the
context and keep the plain file-cache view. A re-render of an incompatible
chapter promotes with replace=True, which drops the now-stale timeline and
waveform sidecars in the same transaction as the WAV. The controller probes the
identity through submission_context_for(voice, report=False) — read-only, so a
chapter-list rebuild never puts a refusal on the user's error banner — and
refreshChapters() re-reads the book after a profile/language change so both the
ready badges and the statuses (and therefore "render all" coverage) follow the
new identity; a render in flight keeps its live "rendering" status. Renders are
snapshotted at submission (_render_context) and credited to the chapter only
through the persist job's own (book_id, index) key, so a render started before
the previous chapter landed cannot be credited with it.

Subtitle: render_fingerprint adds a "context" key ONLY when an identity is
present, so every stored project keeps the hash it always had (pinned by a
golden-digest test) and the legacy migration compares the pre-context
fingerprint of the requested inputs. SubtitleProject.context round-trips through
project.json; cached_render accepts an exact fingerprint match or a stored
context-less render whose pre-context fingerprint still matches while a VieNeu
identity is asking. render() re-derives the project for the CURRENT identity
before the cache check and, when the stored track belongs to another engine,
drops the previous render's measured alignment (total_ms/stats/adjusted) so the
surface never presents it as current.
